export const BUSINESS_ARTICLES = [
  {slug:"busy-but-not-making-money",title:"I'm busy but I'm not making money",question:"Why am I fully booked and still broke?",answer:"Almost always because your hourly rate is set from what you hope customers will pay rather than what the workshop costs to run. Work out your true cost per bench hour first: overheads plus the wage you need, divided by the hours you can actually charge for. Most upholsterers can only bill 55 to 65 per cent of the hours they work, because stripping, collecting, quoting and buying materials are not billable. Being fully booked at a rate below that number just means losing money faster.",section:"Problems Nobody Talks About",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/busy-but-not-making-money"},
  {slug:"can-you-make-a-living-from-upholstery",title:"Can you make a living from upholstery?",question:"Is upholstery a viable career?",answer:"Yes, but almost certainly not in the way you are picturing. Very few people make a living from one kind of upholstery. Thirty-four years in, my own workshop rests on four separate trainings \u2014 traditional work, modern methods, vehicle trimming and antique restoration \u2014 and the money comes from whichever of those is busy that month. Right now a good share of it is campervan conversion work, a market that did not exist when I started. The trade pays. It pays people who keep adding to what they can do.",section:"Starting Out",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/can-you-make-a-living-from-upholstery"},
  {slug:"caravan-and-holiday-park-work",title:"Caravan and holiday park work",question:"Is holiday park maintenance work worth getting into?",answer:"It is maintenance, not upholstery in the way most of us mean the word. Holiday parks want their existing furniture kept in service as long as possible \u2014 zips replaced, cushions renewed to crib 5, sofas rewebbed after a season of hard use. The model is a monthly visit with a van fitted out as a mobile workshop, working through whatever the park has collected since you were last there. A day can bring in over a thousand pounds. Getting approved took us a year. The real prize is that it fills the months when nothing else does.",section:"Working With the Trade",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/caravan-and-holiday-park-work"},
  {slug:"charging-for-estimates",title:"Should I charge for estimates?",question:"Is it reasonable to charge for quoting a job?",answer:"Charge for anything that requires travel or takes real time, and give free quotes from photographs. A site visit costs you two hours and a tank of fuel whether or not you win the work, and giving that away means paying for the privilege of being compared with three other workshops. The usual arrangement is: photo quotes free, home visits charged, and the visit fee credited against the job if it goes ahead.",section:"Quoting Jobs",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/charging-for-estimates"},
  {slug:"customer-supplied-fabric",title:"Customer's own fabric",question:"Should I upholster in fabric the customer supplies?",answer:"You can, but set the terms in writing first. You cannot guarantee cloth you did not source, you lose the fabric margin that forms part of a job's profit, and in the UK you remain responsible for the finished piece meeting fire regulations regardless of who bought the cover. Check its suitability and fire evidence before accepting it, price the labour accordingly, and put in writing that faults, shortfalls and wear in customer-supplied material are not your responsibility.",section:"Dealing With Customers",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/customer-supplied-fabric"},
  {slug:"explaining-what-upholstery-costs",title:"Explaining why upholstery costs what it does",question:"How do I justify the price when a customer can buy a new sofa for less?",answer:"Do not justify it \u2014 explain what they are buying. A new sofa at the same price is a different product: a stapled softwood frame with a five-year life, against a hardwood frame rebuilt to last another thirty. Talk about the frame, the hours and what happens underneath, not about how hard you work. And show them rather than telling them: a photograph of their own chair in the fabric they are considering ends more hesitation than any amount of explanation.",section:"Dealing With Customers",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/explaining-what-upholstery-costs"},
  {slug:"getting-your-first-customers",title:"Getting your first customers",question:"How do I find work when nobody knows I exist?",answer:"Start with the people who already send work to upholsterers rather than with the public. Antique dealers, interior designers, furniture restorers, auction houses and established upholsterers with full diaries all have jobs they cannot or will not take. Set up a Google Business Profile the same week, photograph every piece you finish properly, and ask every satisfied customer for a review while they are still standing in front of the chair. Advertising is rarely the answer; visibility and a portfolio are.",section:"Getting Customers",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/getting-your-first-customers"},
  {slug:"how-much-should-an-upholsterer-charge",title:"How much should an upholsterer charge?",question:"How do I work out my hourly rate?",answer:"Build it up from your own costs rather than copying a competitor. Take your annual overheads, add the wage you need, add a margin for tax and reinvestment, then divide by your realistically billable hours \u2014 usually 55 to 65 per cent of the hours you work. That gives your minimum viable rate. UK workshops in 2026 commonly land between \u00a345 and \u00a390 an hour depending on region and the class of work; a rate copied from someone with different premises and different overheads tells you nothing useful.",section:"Pricing & Profit",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/how-much-should-an-upholsterer-charge"},
  {slug:"insurance-for-an-upholstery-workshop",title:"Insurance for an upholstery workshop",question:"What insurance does an upholsterer actually need?",answer:"More than most workshops carry, and in a different shape than they expect. Public liability is the one everybody buys \u2014 but it usually excludes damage to property in your care, custody and control, which is exactly what a workshop full of customers' furniture is. That gap is the one most upholsterers do not know they have. We started on \u00a31m public liability, went to \u00a35m when a holiday park contract required it, and added more again for teaching. Tools are covered both in the workshop and on site.",section:"Money & Finance",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/insurance-for-an-upholstery-workshop"},
  {slug:"moving-into-campervan-work",title:"Moving into campervan work",question:"How does a furniture upholsterer get into campervan trimming?",answer:"Find the converters near you and ask. They are small businesses that are good at joinery, electrics and plumbing and usually have no upholstery capability at all, so many of them drive a long way to get cushions made. I found mine through a message in a local Facebook group and had a reply in two minutes. The work is bespoke cushions made to templates, it peaks from March to June, and a full set with the cab seats to match is around two days and a thousand pounds in labour. The techniques are close to furniture work. The materials are not.",section:"Working With the Trade",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/moving-into-campervan-work"},
  {slug:"saying-no-to-a-job",title:"I don't want this job \u2014 how do I say no?",question:"How do I turn down work without damaging my reputation?",answer:"Decline quickly, politely, and without a detailed justification. \"I'm afraid it's not something I can take on\" is a complete sentence and needs no defence. Do not price yourself out instead of refusing, because sometimes people say yes. Do not go quiet and hope they lose interest, because that is what makes people leave reviews. If you can name someone better suited, do \u2014 a good referral turns a refusal into a favour.",section:"Problems Nobody Talks About",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/saying-no-to-a-job"},
  {slug:"subcontracting-for-other-workshops",title:"Subcontracting for other workshops",question:"Should I take subcontract work from other upholsterers?",answer:"Yes, and it is one of the most reliable sources of work in the trade, because manufacturers and busy workshops need capacity they cannot justify employing. Two things make it happen: skills they no longer hold in-house, and volume they cannot cope with. The firm that trained me thirty-four years ago now sends me work regularly \u2014 their own traditional upholsterers retired and the company moved to modern production, so the old models come to me. Price it as trade work, get the terms in writing, and never let one client become most of your turnover.",section:"Working With the Trade",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/subcontracting-for-other-workshops"},
  {slug:"the-contract-i-turned-down",title:"The 76-chair contract I turned down",question:"How do I know when a big contract isn't worth taking?",answer:"Judge it on what it pays per hour after materials, not on the headline value. Seventy-six chairs at \u00a375 each sounds like a serious job until the arithmetic puts it near \u00a310 an hour. Then look at what is actually being asked. If it means covering over existing material you cannot certify, working outside the fire regulations for the building it is going into, or putting your name to frames that will not last, the money has stopped being the point. You would be carrying that liability long after the invoice was paid.",section:"Working With the Trade",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/the-contract-i-turned-down"},
  {slug:"the-maintenance-retainer",title:"The maintenance retainer",question:"How do I stop upholstery income being feast and famine?",answer:"Upholstery income arrives in lumps, and the gaps between them are what break small workshops. A maintenance retainer changes the shape of it rather than the size: a commercial client pays a fixed sum each month by Direct Debit for an agreed number of visits or hours, and you get income that arrives whether or not the phone rings. The strongest version does not sell repairs at all. It sells documented fire compliance, which is something a commercial client legally needs and almost nobody keeps on top of.",section:"Money & Finance",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/the-maintenance-retainer"},
  {slug:"the-physical-toll",title:"The physical toll",question:"What does a career in upholstery do to your body?",answer:"It is a physical trade and the damage is cumulative rather than dramatic. Mine came from piecework \u2014 the same wing chair ten times a day for six months, forty minutes each \u2014 and it gave me carpal tunnel syndrome that woke me every night with two fingers and a thumb numb. Changing my technique did not fix it, because the problem was the volume rather than the method. What fixed it was getting control of my own pace. Thirty-four years on I still work, but if a batch of identical chairs comes in, it comes back.",section:"Problems Nobody Talks About",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/the-physical-toll"},
  {slug:"uncollected-furniture",title:"A customer hasn't collected their furniture",question:"What do I do with finished work nobody comes for?",answer:"Chase it in writing early, and have terms that let you charge storage. Finished furniture sitting in a workshop is money you have already spent and space you cannot use. Write, then write again with a deadline and a storage charge, keeping every message. Do not assume you can simply sell it: in most countries uncollected goods stay the customer's property and disposing of them without following the correct notice procedure exposes you rather than them. Check your own jurisdiction before acting.",section:"Problems Nobody Talks About",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/uncollected-furniture"},
  {slug:"winning-contract-clients",title:"Winning contract clients",question:"How do I get care home, hotel and commercial work?",answer:"Not by cold emailing them, on the evidence. We have sent a lot of approaches to care homes, hotels and facilities management firms and had almost no replies at all. The contract work we actually hold came three other ways: a formal approved-contractor process that took a year, a client who came to us, and one cold email that landed \u2014 and that one was a heritage property with antique furniture, not a chain. The pattern is that commercial buyers reply when nobody else can solve their problem, and ignore you when they already have a supplier.",section:"Working With the Trade",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/winning-contract-clients"},
  {slug:"working-with-your-partner",title:"Working with your partner",question:"Does running an upholstery business with your spouse actually work?",answer:"It can, and ours does, but the arrangement matters more than the goodwill. The single best decision we made was separate spaces \u2014 my wife has her office, I have the workshop, and either of us can disappear when the other is getting on their nerves. She handles the administration and the books and is qualified to do it, which makes her a partner rather than someone roped in to help. Most upholstery workshops fail on the admin rather than the craft, so that division is not a nicety. It is the business.",section:"Growing the Business",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/working-with-your-partner"},
  {slug:"worse-underneath-than-expected",title:"The chair is worse underneath than I expected",question:"What do I do when a job turns out bigger than the quote?",answer:"Stop work, photograph everything, and speak to the customer before you spend another hour on it. A quote covers what could be seen when you gave it; genuinely hidden damage is a variation, not something you absorb. Say what you found, what it will cost, and give them the option to decline. Most people are reasonable when told early and shown a photograph. Almost nobody is reasonable when handed a bill at the end that they never agreed to.",section:"Quoting Jobs",url:"https://www.learntoupholster.com/business/worse-underneath-than-expected"}
];
